tags.description:
Autodesk Platform Services (APS) + Claude AI pipeline for extracting a
professional Bill of Quantities (BOQ) from CAD/BIM building files.

---

## Supported File Types

| Extension | Type    | Format                        | Notes |
|-----------|---------|-------------------------------|-------|
| `.rvt`    | 3D BIM  | Autodesk Revit                | Best for BOQ — full element properties (Category, Family, Type, Volume, Area) |
| `.ifc`    | 3D BIM  | Industry Foundation Classes   | Open BIM standard; rich geometry + classification |
| `.nwd`    | 3D BIM  | Navisworks Document           | Aggregated 3D model with properties |
| `.skp`    | 3D BIM  | SketchUp                      | 3D model with component data |
| `.fbx`    | 3D BIM  | Autodesk FBX                  | 3D exchange format |
| `.obj`    | 3D BIM  | Wavefront OBJ                 | Basic 3D geometry (limited properties) |
| `.dwg`    | 2D CAD  | AutoCAD Drawing               | Layer-based 2D drawings; property richness depends on authoring |
| `.dxf`    | 2D CAD  | Drawing Exchange Format       | AutoCAD interchange format |
| `.dgn`    | 2D CAD  | MicroStation Design            | Bentley 2D/3D drawings |

> **PDF files are NOT supported.** APS renders them as raster images with no property
> database. Use the **AI PDF BOQ** pipeline (`/api/v1/pdf-boq/`) for PDF drawings instead.

---

## 2D / 3D Auto-Detection

The system **automatically detects** whether the uploaded file is a 2D CAD drawing or a
3D BIM model and applies a format-specific extraction strategy:

| Detection Method | Details |
|------------------|---------|
| **File extension** | `.rvt`, `.ifc`, `.nwd`, `.skp`, `.fbx`, `.obj` → always **3D BIM** |
| **APS manifest**   | For ambiguous extensions (`.dwg`, `.dxf`, `.dgn`), checks if translation produced 3D derivatives |
| **Fallback**       | Defaults to **2D CAD** if no 3D signal found |

**3D BIM extraction:** Uses **Category** (e.g. Walls, Floors, Windows) as primary grouping,
extracts Family/Type Name for richer specification, trusts APS-reported units directly.

**2D CAD extraction:** Uses **Layer** as primary grouping, applies mm→m conversion heuristics
(common for Nigerian-authored AutoCAD files), extracts hatch patterns as material indicators.

> **Tip:** Pass the `originalFilename` (e.g. `"Building.rvt"`) in the BOQ submit request body
> so the system can use extension-based detection. If omitted, it falls back to the APS
> manifest analysis which is still reliable but slightly less deterministic for `.dwg` files.

---

## Frontend Integration Guide

### Step-by-step flow:

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  1. POST /upload            Upload file → get URN          │
│         ↓                                                  │
│  2. GET /status/:urn        Poll every 5-10s               │
│         ↓ (status === "success")                           │
│  3. POST /boq/:urn          Submit BOQ job → get jobId     │
│         ↓                                                  │
│  4. GET /jobs/:jobId        Poll every 3-5s                │
│         ↓ (status === "completed")                         │
│  5. Display BOQ from data.result                           │
│         ↓                                                  │
│  6. (Optional) PATCH /jobs/:jobId     Edit BOQ result      │
│  7. (Optional) GET /jobs/:jobId/pdf   Download as PDF      │
│  8. (Optional) POST /jobs/:jobId/create-project            │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Frontend pseudo-code:

```javascript
// 1. Upload — store the returned URN and originalFilename
const upload = await POST('/bim/upload', formData);  // multipart
const urn = upload.data.urn;
const filename = file.name;  // e.g. "Building.rvt"

// 2. Poll translation status
let status;
do {
  await sleep(5000);
  status = await GET(`/bim/status/${urn}`);
} while (['pending', 'inprogress'].includes(status.data.status));
if (status.data.status !== 'success') throw new Error('Translation failed');

// 3. Submit BOQ job — pass originalFilename for 2D/3D detection
const job = await POST(`/bim/boq/${urn}`, {
  documentHint: 'Residential tower, Lagos',
  originalFilename: filename,
});
const jobId = job.data.jobId;

// 4. Poll job status
let result;
do {
  await sleep(3000);
  result = await GET(`/bim/jobs/${jobId}`);
} while (['pending', 'processing'].includes(result.data.status));
if (result.data.status !== 'completed') throw new Error(result.data.errorMessage);

// 5. Display/edit the BOQ
displayBoq(result.data.result);  // { projectTitle, sections, generalNotes }

// 6. (Optional) Save user edits
await PATCH(`/bim/jobs/${jobId}`, editedBoqResult);

// 7. (Optional) Download PDF
const pdf = await GET(`/bim/jobs/${jobId}/pdf`, { responseType: 'blob' });

// 8. (Optional) Create project
await POST(`/bim/jobs/${jobId}/create-project`, { name: 'My Project' });
```

AI-powered Bill of Quantities extraction from **PDF construction drawings**.

Claude reads every page of the PDF visually (no OCR — direct vision model)
and returns a structured, professional BOQ. This works with scanned drawings,
architectural plans, structural details, and specification documents.

---

## When to Use This vs BIM Pipeline

| Scenario | Use |
|----------|-----|
| You have the original BIM/CAD file (RVT, IFC, DWG, etc.) | Use **BIM Pipeline** (`/api/v1/bim/`) — property data yields more accurate quantities |
| You only have a PDF drawing (scan, export, or print) | Use **PDF BOQ** (`/api/v1/pdf-boq/`) — Claude reads the drawing visually |
| Client sent you a PDF specification document | Use **PDF BOQ** |

---

## Frontend Integration Guide

### Step-by-step flow:

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  1. POST /generate            Upload PDF → get jobId       │
│         ↓                                                  │
│  2. GET /jobs/:jobId          Poll every 3-5s              │
│         ↓ (status === "completed")                         │
│  3. Display BOQ from data.result                           │
│         ↓                                                  │
│  4. (Optional) PATCH /jobs/:jobId     Save user edits      │
│  5. (Optional) GET /jobs/:jobId/pdf   Download as PDF      │
│  6. (Optional) POST /jobs/:jobId/create-project            │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Frontend pseudo-code:

```javascript
// 1. Upload PDF
const formData = new FormData();
formData.append('file', pdfFile);
formData.append('documentHint', 'Residential development, 4 floors, Lagos');
const upload = await POST('/pdf-boq/generate', formData);  // multipart
const jobId = upload.data.jobId;

// 2. Poll job status
let result;
do {
  await sleep(3000);
  result = await GET(`/pdf-boq/jobs/${jobId}`);
} while (['pending', 'processing'].includes(result.data.status));
if (result.data.status !== 'completed') throw new Error(result.data.errorMessage);

// 3. Display the BOQ
displayBoq(result.data.result);  // { projectTitle, sections, generalNotes }

// 4. (Optional) Save user edits
await PATCH(`/pdf-boq/jobs/${jobId}`, editedBoqResult);

// 5. (Optional) Download PDF
const pdf = await GET(`/pdf-boq/jobs/${jobId}/pdf`, { responseType: 'blob' });

// 6. (Optional) Create project
await POST(`/pdf-boq/jobs/${jobId}/create-project`, { name: 'My Project' });
```

---

## Limits

- **Max file size:** 32 MB
- **Max pages:** ~100 pages (Claude vision API limit)
- **Accepted format:** PDF only (`.pdf`)
- **Job expiry:** 7 days (create a project to persist permanently)

get.description:
Renders the stored BOQ result as a professional A4 PDF and streams it as a
file download. The PDF includes a formatted table with sections, work items,
quantities, units, rates, and totals.

**Prerequisites:**
- Job must be in `"completed"` status.
- If you've saved user edits via `PATCH /jobs/:jobId`, the PDF will reflect
  the updated data.

**Frontend implementation:**
```javascript
const response = await fetch(`/api/v1/bim/jobs/${jobId}/pdf`, {
  headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
});
const blob = await response.blob();
const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
window.open(url);  // or trigger download
```

